WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 10.6 · Legal, Compliance, Policies & Risk
Data protection and privacy

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Build data protection in from the first sign-up: it is far easier than fixing it later.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:25.170
Data protection means handling personal information lawfully, fairly and securely. Laws differ by country, but most share the same principles, and they apply from your very first sign-up.

4
00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:26.170
Principles most laws share

5
00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:37.500
Clear purpose: Collect personal information for a clear and lawful reason, and only what you need. For example: The bakery asks trainees for an emergency contact, not their school records.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:48.089
Accurate and secure: Keep data correct, protected and visible only to people who need it. For example: Trainee forms go into a password-protected folder, not onto a shared phone.

7
00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:57.937
Limited time: Hold data only as long as necessary, then delete it. For example: The bakery deletes trainee contact details one year after the traineeship ends.

8
00:00:57.937 --> 00:01:13.430
Openness and rights: Tell people what you do with their data, and respect their rights to see, correct or delete it. For example: A privacy notice on the trainee sign-up form explains how to see or delete their data.

9
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.430
Why build it in now

10
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:20.474
1. It is far easier to build in now than to fix later.

11
00:01:20.474 --> 00:01:26.519
2. Data about children, health or ethnicity needs extra care from the start.

12
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:33.563
3. A breach can damage trust you have only just begun to build.

13
00:01:33.963 --> 00:01:34.563
What you will build

14
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:41.719
1. A data inventory: what you hold, why, where, who sees it and for how long.

15
00:01:41.719 --> 00:01:48.133
2. A privacy notice, consent where needed, a retention schedule and a breach plan.

16
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:54.437
3. Data-sharing agreements with partners, and training for everyone handling data.

17
00:01:54.837 --> 00:01:55.437
What to avoid

18
00:01:55.437 --> 00:01:59.259
Collecting extra data “just in case”.

19
00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:04.563
Keeping contact lists on personal phones or open shared folders.

20
00:02:04.563 --> 00:02:10.867
Not checking local duties, such as registering with an authority.

21
00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:13.267
You build your data inventory and list your privacy actions.

22
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:17.267
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:21.867
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
